text:
alex: Heads-up — customer acct_0142 submitted a support escalation: they were charged twice for one generate call. They provided request logs, shows two POSTs to /v1/generate within ~150ms. Looks correlated to TS/Node SDK usage (node 20, sdk v1.4.3).

maria: Oof. Do we know if they're setting idempotency keys? Any retries configured? :thinking:

alex: Not using idempotency keys on their side. SDK config they shared: `retries: 2`, default backoff. They said they didn't do any custom stream cloning. Minimal logs attached below.

mike: Can you paste the relevant log snippet?

alex: Log fragment: 
```
[2068012468] INFO request start POST /v1/generate id=req_ABC123 user=acct_0142
[2068012469] ERROR network transient error: socket hang up (code ECONNRESET)
[2068012469] WARN retry attempt=1 for req_ABC123
[2068012470] INFO request start POST /v1/generate id=req_ABC123 user=acct_0142
[2068012470] INFO response 200 OK id=req_ABC123
```

mike: That pattern matches the known "body replay" problem when the SDK attempts retries while a streamed body or an AbortController signal is reused. If the underlying HTTP client re-sends the body, the upstream will see two POSTs. We've seen this surface when undici/node internal stream state isn't rewindable.

maria: Customer said they hit this from a serverless handler where they reuse an AbortController instance across requests (they pass a global signal). Could that cause duplicate sends?

mike: Reusing the same AbortController instance across sequential requests can cause odd timing where the abort signal is in a terminal state or gets reattached and we end up with overlap in backoff/retry timing. The two common bugs: (1) reusing a non-cloneable request body stream across retries, (2) AbortController handoff between attempts where undici replays the socket.

jordan: Can we get a minimal repro? If we can reproduce locally with node 20 + undici 5.x + sdk v1.4.3 then we can confirm. Alex, can you ask them for a tiny harness?

alex: They sent a gist with a small repro harness (trimmed):
```
// repro.ts
import fetch from 'undici';
import { AbortController } from 'abort-controller';
const ctrl = new AbortController(); // reused across calls (bug)
const body = JSON.stringify({ prompt: 'hi' });

async function call() {
  // SDK wraps fetch and sets retries=2 by default
  await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/generate', {
    method: 'POST',
    body,
    signal: ctrl.signal,
    headers: { 'content-type': 'application/json' }
  });
}

// simulate two sequential requests inside same handler
await call();
await call();
```

mike: Perfect. That repro will show the second call sometimes being replayed when the first attempt has a transient network failure and the SDK triggers a retry path that doesn't properly clone the body or reset undici internal stream state.

jordan: Suggested mitigations: 1) short-term: ask customer to set `retries: 0` or send an idempotency-key header on POSTs; 2) medium-term: recommend upgrading to SDK v1.4.4 which contains the fix for replaying bodies on retry (PR #6723); 3) we should add a note in docs about not reusing AbortController instances across independent requests.

maria: Can we confirm whether v1.4.4 is published and which undici version it pins? Also need to know if Dedicated/private customers will get the fix automatically.

jordan: v1.4.4 pins undici 5.5.1 and includes a retry wrapper that clones bodies for common primitives and disallows retry for non-rewindable streams. Dedicated customers on pinned stacks will need a rollout; migrating infra images with the SDK bump is in flight (ENG-ROLL/324). Private customers must upgrade their client package.

mike: For immediate triage I recommended we patch the hosted gateway to treat repeated POSTs within 500ms without idempotency-key as potential dupes and route them to a dedup queue for billing review. That's an ops mitigation only.

alex: Customer applied temporary `retries: 0` and added an idempotency-key header while we coordinated. They confirmed no further duplicates in their next 100 calls.

maria: Can you open an internal incident and link the customer case? Also add steps to reproduce to the ticket so eng-runtime can prioritize.

jordan: Created INC-4321 and assigned to eng-runtime (jordan). Linked PR #6723, repro harness, and customer logs.

mike: Follow-ups: (a) add an SDK warning/error when users pass a shared AbortController or non-serializable body and `retries > 0`, (b) add a test in CI that simulates transient socket failures and verifies we do not double-send bodies, (c) add a short FAQ blurb about idempotency-key usage for POST endpoints.

alex: I'll send the customer the mitigation steps and the upgrade ETA. Thanks all :thumbsup:

support-bot: Incident INC-4321 created by jordan — https://redwood.internal/incidents/INC-4321 (priority P2).
